---
/* The general-purpose map: the same engine the hurricane page uses, with no
   scientific framing on top of it.

   The difference between this page and that one is its preset and its home
   bounds — bathymetry, surface flow and a shoreline, opened on the whole
   ocean rather than a basin. Everything else is one switch away in the layer
   list, and the reader's choices are kept for the session. */
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import AssetMap from '../components/AssetMap.astro';
import UtcClock from '../components/UtcClock.astro';

const title = 'Visualization';
const description =
  'An interactive map of the global ocean: bathymetry, modelled currents at ' +
  'two depths, sea-surface temperature and salinity, seafloor contours, ' +
  'maritime boundaries, and the uncrewed platforms reporting from it.';
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} wide>
  <article class="container wide">
    <!-- The heading is the eyebrow: "Ocean data visualization" is one phrase,
         and the map below it is what the page is for. It stays an h1 — the
         document still needs a heading, it is just set as a label rather than
         a banner. -->
    <div class="topline">
      <h1 class="eyebrow">Ocean data visualization</h1>
      <UtcClock />
    </div>

    <!-- The preset: what a reader who has never been here sees. Sea-surface
         temperature under the surface flow, over the seafloor, with the
         shorelines, the borders and the grid for reference — the whole ocean
         at once, which is the picture this page exists to show.

         **The isobaths are the expensive one and it is worth knowing by how
         much.** Measured against the live host: SST 47 KB gzipped, the
         currents 117, the borders 68 — and the deep isobath tier 2,916. So this
         preset is 3.07 MB on a first load and 95% of it is the contours,
         which at globe zoom are faint lines under a saturated colour scale.
         Dropping Isobaths takes the first load to about 160 KB and changes
         very little on screen; it is in because it was asked for, not
         because the arithmetic favours it.

         A comment cannot go between the attributes below. A JSX one there
         swallows the rest of the element — nine errors, none of them
         pointing at the comment — which CLAUDE.md already records from the
         last time. -->
    <AssetMap
      storageKey="visualization-map-view"
      label="Interactive map of the global ocean"
      home={[[-60, -175], [70, 175]]}
      layers={[
        'SST (ESPC)',
        'Currents at 0m (ESPC)',
        'Isobaths',
        'Coastline',
        'Country & state borders',
        'Lat/lon grid',
      ]}
    />

    <!-- Collapsed by default, and native <details> rather than a script: the
         page is the map, and prose above the fold pushes it down on a phone
         for a reader who has already worked out what a layer switcher is.
         Open by keyboard, findable by browser search, and works with no JS —
         which is what the rest of this site does too. -->
    <details class="aside">
      <summary>Quick start</summary>
      <ul>
        <li>
          The layer list, top right, holds the basemaps and every overlay. Only
          one scalar field and one current depth can show at a time — two of
          either over the same water cannot be told apart.
        </li>
        <li>
          Right-click, or press and hold on a touchscreen, to read the position,
          the seafloor depth and the current wherever you point. With a
          temperature or salinity layer on, that too; with maritime boundaries
          on, whose waters they are.
        </li>
        <li>
          The 📏 button measures distance and bearing along as many legs as you
          click. Escape clears it.
        </li>
        <li>
          Under the map: the colour scale, its range, how strongly the depth
          contours show through, and the <strong>forecast hour</strong>. Reset
          puts everything back.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Your own overlay.</strong> Load a KMZ or KML — a survey plan, a
          station grid, an operating area, a georeferenced chart — and it is
          drawn over the top and kept in this browser for next time. It stays on
          your machine; nothing is uploaded anywhere.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </details>

    <details class="aside">
      <summary>Data sources</summary>
      <p>
        The same feeds as the <a href="/observations/hurricanes/">hurricane
        page</a>, which describes each one: NHC storm forecasts, gliders from
        four national data centres, NOAA saildrones, the Argo array, the US Navy
        ESPC-D-V02 forecast for currents, temperature and salinity, NOAA OISST
        for observed temperature, ECMWF IFS open data for 10 m wind, GEBCO for
        bathymetry and depth contours, EMODnet for the shoreline, and Marine
        Regions for maritime boundaries.
        Everything but the seafloor is refetched hourly.
      </p>
    </details>

  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .topline {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: baseline;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: var(--space-sm);
  }

  .aside {
    max-width: 65ch;
    margin-top: var(--space-md);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
  }

  .aside summary {
    padding: var(--space-xs) 0;
    cursor: pointer;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  /* The marker is the only thing saying these open, so it has to stay put
     when the summary is styled as an eyebrow. */
  .aside summary:hover,
  .aside summary:focus-visible {
    color: var(--text);
  }

  .aside[open] summary {
    color: var(--text);
  }

  ul {
    max-width: 65ch;
  }

  li {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-xs);
  }
</style>
